  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}}$ is $y=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{5}{x},$ $\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}},$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0+0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$",
